From 920c82b1db470dfaf470c495274eb9680e7ac9f3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Sergi=E3=83=BB=E3=82=BB=E3=83=AB=E3=82=B8?=
 <xerpi.g.12@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 14:17:54 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 20/52] kernel: fix more sync primitives behavior found on
 hardware (#4228)

* kernel: wake one waiter when signaling a condition

Signaling a condition woke every waiting thread, like signaling all of
them does. It now wakes only the first thread in the queue, as a Vita
does.

* kernel: release the mutexes of a thread that ends

A thread that ended while holding a mutex kept it forever, and its
waiters waited until they timed out. On a Vita the mutex is unlocked
when its owner ends and goes to the next waiter, so do that.

* kernel: write back the time left after a message pipe wait

Message pipe sends and receives that waited never wrote the time left
back to their timeout, so one that timed out still had all of it. They
now write it back like the other waits.

* kernel: keep a thread's exit status like a Vita does

A thread's exit status is now DORMANT until it first starts,
NOT_DORMANT while it runs, and then the value it returned or exited
with. The thread info reports it for every thread, not just ended ones,
a wait for the end of a thread that never started gets it at once, and
getting the exit status of a thread that never started fails with
DORMANT. A callback's result no longer replaces the exit status, and
sceKernelGetThreadExitStatus calls the thread manager's version instead
of keeping its own copy.

* kernel: fix the errors for starting a running thread or deleting oneself

Starting a thread that isn't dormant now fails with NOT_DORMANT instead
of RUNNING, and a thread deleting itself fails with ILLEGAL_THREAD_ID
instead of NOT_DORMANT, as on a Vita.

* kernel: report CPU affinity masks as they were set

A thread created with an affinity mask of 0 reported every user core,
while on a Vita it reports 0, and so does changing its affinity. Getting
the affinity now returns the mask as it was set. A main thread whose
process doesn't give one gets every user core, which is what a Vita
reports for it. sceKernelGetThreadCpuAffinityMask2 still reports every
user core for a mask of 0.

* kernel: implement sceKernelGetThreadStackFreeSize

It was unimplemented. It now returns the whole stack for a dormant
thread, and the space below the stack pointer for one that started, or
fails with UNKNOWN_THREAD_ID for a UID that isn't a thread.

* kernel: fix the errors for thread affinity and exit status arguments

Creating a thread with an affinity mask that has cores games can't use
now fails with ILLEGAL_CPU_AFFINITY_MASK instead of
INVALID_CPU_AFFINITY, and getting an exit status into a NULL pointer
fails with ILLEGAL_ADDR, as on a Vita.

* kernel: copy as much of a thread's info as the game asks for

sceKernelGetThreadInfo failed for any size but the struct's. Like the
other info getters, it now copies the start of the struct, as many bytes
as the size the game sets, and fails with NO_MEMORY for a size past the
struct. The fields it doesn't fill in are now 0.

* kernel: count timer time like a Vita does

A timer kept only the clock time it was last started or stopped at, so
a new timer wasn't at 0, a stopped one went on counting and setting the
time didn't move the count. It now keeps a count that starts at 0,
stops while the timer is stopped and goes on from there when it starts
again, and setting the time replaces it. Stopping a timer returns 1
when it stopped it and 0 when it was stopped already, the other way
around before. The base is now the process time the count runs from,
and sceKernelSetTimerTimeWide returns the whole 64-bit old time.

* kernel: give a failed timer poll no bits

A timer poll or wait that didn't get the event still got the timer bit
as its result. It now gets 0, as on a Vita, and only a take that gets
the event gets the timer bit.

* kernel: let a waiting writer go first on a reader/writer lock

Unlocking a reader/writer lock handed it to the waiters in queue order,
so readers that waited before a writer went first, and new readers got
in while a writer waited. Like on a Vita, an unlock now gives the lock
to the first waiting writer, or to every waiting reader if no writer
waits, and a read lock fails or waits while a writer waits.

* kernel: order message pipe waiters by the pipe's own attributes

Message pipes ordered their receivers by the thread priority attribute
the other objects use, which creating a message pipe refuses, and their
senders always in FIFO order. Receivers now queue by priority with
attribute 0x2, as on a Vita, and senders with 0x1.

* kernel: report an event flag's initial pattern in its info

The event flag info gave the current pattern as the initial one too.
It now gives the pattern the event flag was created with.

* kernel: round message pipe buffers up to 4 KiB

A message pipe got a buffer of exactly the size it was created with.
On a Vita the buffer is rounded up to a multiple of 4 KiB, which its
info reports, so a 256 byte pipe holds 4 KiB.

* kernel: implement sceKernelGetMsgPipeInfo

It was unimplemented. It now reports a message pipe's name, attributes,
buffer and free sizes and how many threads wait to send or receive,
like the other info getters.

* kernel: report a callback's notification count in its info

The callback info left the notification count at 0. It now gives how
many times the callback was notified since it last ran.

* kernel: fill lightweight mutex and condition work areas like a Vita

Games can read these work areas, so they now hold what a Vita leaves in
them. Creating a lightweight mutex or condition clears its work area
first, and unlocking a lightweight mutex writes its new owner and count
to it. A lightweight condition's work area holds its mutex's UID after
its own, and both become 0xFFFFFFFF when it is deleted.
sceKernelDeleteLwCond now calls the thread manager's version instead of
keeping its own copy.

* ci: run the latest VitaAutotests

Move AUTOTESTS_REF to the VitaAutotests commit that adds two tests, a
thread that holds a read lock reading again while a writer waits, which
passes, and changing the priority of a waiting thread, which still
fails.

(cherry picked from commit 20df7f4ea7e2e7659c8c1e25b3c47e3063b69b57)
